Aim: Analyze Recursive Algorithm Mathematically & implement in C.
Theory: Recursive algorithms solve a problem by having a function call itself on smaller subproblems, combined with a base case that terminates the recursion (e.g., computing a factorial). Their time complexity is analyzed by setting up a recurrence relation and solving it using methods such as substitution, the recursion-tree method, or the Master Theorem. For factorial, T(n) = T(n-1) + O(1), which resolves to O(n).
Algorithm:
FACTORIAL(n):
1. If n = 0, return 1 (base case)
2. Else, return n * FACTORIAL(n-1)
3. Stop
Program:
/* Practical 23: Analyze Recursive Algorithm Mathematically & Implement in C (CO4)
 * Example: Factorial using Recursion
 * Recurrence Relation: T(n) = T(n-1) + O(1), T(0) = O(1)
 * Solving by back-substitution: T(n) = O(n)
 */
#include <stdio.h>
long long factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) return 1;      /* Base case: O(1) */
    return n * factorial(n - 1);          /* Recursive case: T(n-1) + O(1) */
}
int main() {
